+## Ownership maintenance in the Murage vendor build
+
+`close-only --session NAME` connects to an existing daemon. `launch-group --owner-file PATH --status` reads ownership without starting a browser. On Windows, `launch-group --owner-file PATH --stop` opens the recorded named job and confirms zero active processes before reporting exhaustion. Unix external stop is refused; only the owning daemon may stop its held children. Maintenance commands are intentionally absent from browser-automation MCP tools.
+
+`close --all`, `dashboard stop`, and the dashboard stop endpoint require an owned stop channel or job. A standalone dashboard without an ownership record remains available for its owner to close; a PID file alone never authorizes a signal.
